The `.wasm` modules in this directory and the expected results in
`manifest.txt` are generated by `tools/wasm-simd-fixture.py`, which is in this
repository. Their provenance has two halves and only one of them is external.

The modules themselves are this repository's: the tool writes each case as
WebAssembly text and assembles it to binary. The assembler is the one in the
`wasmtime` package, but what it produces is the encoding the WebAssembly core
specification fixes for that text, and any conforming assembler produces the
same bytes.

The expected results are `wasmtime`'s. Each module is executed by that engine
and the sixteen result bytes recorded, and `wasm_simd_vectors.rs` requires this
crate's host to produce the same bytes. That is the point of the directory:
`wasm.rs` implements some 230 lane-wise SIMD instructions, and an independent
engine's answers are the only thing that can tell a correct implementation from
one that is merely self-consistent.

Bytecode Alliance's `wasmtime` is distributed under the Apache License 2.0 with
the LLVM exception. No wasmtime source is copied here — the dependency is on
running it to produce these numbers, which is why it lives in `tools/` outside
the crate's zero-dependency boundary, and why re-generating the directory needs
`pip install wasmtime` while building and testing this crate does not.

One case is deliberately compared more loosely than byte equality, and the
reason is in the specification rather than in convenience: when an arithmetic
operation returns NaN, WebAssembly leaves the payload and the sign
unconstrained, so two conforming engines may legitimately differ there. The
comparison treats a lane where *both* engines produced a NaN as agreement, and
nothing else — `abs`, `neg` and `copysign` are exempted from even that, because
their behaviour on a NaN is specified exactly as a bit operation. The reasoning
is written out at `nan_equivalent` in the test.
